      "By the time Kant started working, philosophy had split into two armed camps, and each had talked itself into a wall. On one side stood the **rationalists** (Descartes, Spinoza, Leibniz, and the German textbook tradition Kant grew up inside, era 3). Their conviction was that *pure reason*, thinking carefully enough on its own, could deliver real knowledge of the deepest things: that God exists, that the soul is immortal, that the universe has such-and-such ultimate structure, all of it spun out of reason without ever checking against experience. Their best case is not silly: mathematics really does seem to work exactly this way. A geometer proves things about triangles by pure thought, never measuring a single physical triangle, and the proofs hold with a certainty no amount of measuring could give. If reason can do *that* for triangles, why not for God and the soul? That was the rationalist bet.",
      "On the other side stood the **empiricists** (Locke, Berkeley, and above all the Scotsman David Hume, era 3). Their conviction was the reverse: every scrap of knowledge comes in through the senses, and a mind with no experience is a blank page. Their best case is just as strong, because Hume pushed it to a genuinely terrifying conclusion. If all our ideas come from experience, Hume asked, then where do we get the idea of *cause*, of one thing *making* another happen? One billiard ball strikes another. The first ball moves, then the second ball moves. The observer never sees the *making*, the necessity, the invisible force that supposedly connects them. All that is ever observed is one thing, then another thing. So our towering confidence that every event must have a cause, the bedrock assumption underneath all of Newton's physics, turns out to be something we never actually observe. It is just a *habit* of expectation, built up because we have seen one-thing-then-another so many times. On Hume's account, science is not knowledge of how nature is bound together by necessary law. It is a remarkably reliable betting habit. That is the dead end: if Hume is right, the most successful body of knowledge humans have ever produced, Newtonian physics, isn't knowledge at all.",
      "This is the wreckage Kant walked into, and he later said, with unusual frankness, that it was Hume who \"first interrupted my dogmatic slumber,\" who shook him out of the comfortable rationalist assumption that reason just naturally fits the world. Kant could not unsee Hume's point. But he also could not accept Hume's conclusion: he was a working scientist's philosopher, he revered Newton, and a universe in which causation was a mere mental habit was, to him, intolerable. So he did something neither camp had thought to do. Both sides had assumed the same thing without noticing it, that to know an object, the mind must *conform to* the object, lining itself up with how the thing really is out there. Kant proposed turning that assumption inside out. *What if the object conforms to the mind?* What if the basic structure we find in nature (that everything is in space and time, that every event has a cause) is not something we read *off* the world but something the mind itself *imposes on* the world in the act of experiencing it?",
      "He compared the move, deliberately, to Copernicus. Astronomers before Copernicus tried to explain the motions of the heavens by assuming the stars revolved around the stationary observer, and got tangled in impossible knots. Copernicus broke the knot by reversing it: let the *observer* move, and the stars hold still. In Kant's own words: \"When he found that he could make no progress by assuming that all the heavenly bodies revolved round the spectator, he reversed the process, and tried the experiment of assuming that the spectator revolved, while the stars remained at rest.\" Kant proposed the same reversal for knowledge. Stop assuming the mind revolves around the object. Try assuming the object revolves around the mind. The concrete payoff he was reaching for, in his words: \"If the intuition must conform to the nature of the objects, I do not see how we can know anything of them *à priori*. If, on the other hand, the object conforms to the nature of our faculty of intuition, I can then easily conceive the possibility of such an *à priori* knowledge.\"",
      "That is the break, and everything that follows hangs off it. If the mind supplies the basic structure of experience, then Hume is answered: causation *is* necessary and universal, not because we discover it out in the world, but because the mind imposes it on everything that could ever count as experience, so it is guaranteed to hold for the whole world we can ever encounter. Science is saved. But there is a price, and it is steep, and it is the other half of the break. If we only ever know the world *as our minds have structured it*, then we never know the world as it is *in itself*, behind our structuring. Reason can map experience with perfect rigor, yet reason can say nothing whatever about God, the soul, or the universe-as-a-whole, because those lie beyond any possible experience. The same move that rescues science fences off **metaphysics** (the attempt to know reality beyond anything the senses can show: God, the soul, the world as a whole), and fences it off forever. And then, in the turn that makes Kant Kant, that very fence, by clearing the ground of false knowledge-claims, leaves an empty room where something else can stand: not knowledge, but a moral law the reader will meet in Chapter 5. \"I must,\" Kant wrote, \"abolish *knowledge*, to make room for *belief*.\""
