          "p": "The machine opens here and the gears come into view; it is the hardest part of the Critique. Kant unglossed is genuinely unreadable; Kant with examples is merely difficult. The driving question of the whole *Critique of Pure Reason* can be put in five words: *how is knowledge in advance possible?* That sounds like jargon; it is not. Kant noticed that we possess knowledge that is somehow true *in advance* of checking the world, and yet *informative* about the world, and on the standard pictures of his day, that combination should be impossible. Pinning down why it's possible is the engine of everything that follows. First, two distinctions, because the impossible thing lives exactly where they cross."
