          "p": "**Distinction one: how you come to know it.** Some things you know only by *checking*, by going and looking. \"It is raining outside,\" \"this cup holds coffee,\" \"there are nine planets.\" Kant calls this knowledge *a posteriori*: knowledge that comes *after*, and depends on, experience. Other things you know *without* checking any particular case, because they couldn't be otherwise: you don't need to survey bachelors to know they're unmarried, or count to be sure 7 + 5 = 12 for every set of seven and five things in the universe. Kant calls this *a priori*: knowledge that holds *prior to*, independent of, experience, and that carries a stamp experience can never give, the stamp of *necessity* (it *must* be so) and *universality* (it holds *always*, no exceptions). Experience can only ever tell you how things *have been so far*; it can never deliver a *must*."
